course: dictionary
lesson: 11165
title: The windpipe
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Your windpipe is the tube in your throat that carries air. Air comes in through your nose or mouth, goes down the windpipe, and reaches your lungs. Its science name is the trachea.
explain: «When you breathe in, air goes down your windpipe into two lungs, which are full of tiny air bags.» So the windpipe is the road the air travels along, and the lungs are where the air goes next.
explain: The windpipe sits in front of your food pipe. Food and drink should go down the food pipe, not the windpipe. When something goes down the wrong way, it can block the windpipe, which is why you should not talk or run while you eat.
fact: The windpipe is the tube in your throat that carries air from your mouth and nose down to your lungs.
fact: Another science name for the windpipe is the trachea.
fact: «Food stuck in the windpipe» can stop air getting through, and this is called choking.
fact: When you swallow, a flap called the epiglottis covers the windpipe.
fact: The windpipe is in front of the food pipe.
exercise: In the lesson, what does the windpipe carry? | Blood, around the head | *Air, from the nose and mouth down to the lungs | Food, down to the stomach | Water, out of the body
exercise: Which other name means the same as windpipe? | *trachea | food pipe | epiglottis | lungs
exercise: «She gives him a big soft toy instead, because a small part could block his windpipe.» What does block mean here? | Paint over | Tell a joke | Carry a heavy box | *Stop something getting through
test: Why is it dangerous to laugh or talk while you swallow? | Your windpipe gets bigger | It makes the food go faster | It helps the air reach your lungs | *Some drink can slip into the windpipe
test: Where is the windpipe, compared with the food pipe? | Far away, down in your leg | *In front of it | Inside it | Behind it
exam: Which path does air take when you breathe in? | Lungs, then windpipe, then nose | Mouth, then stomach, then lungs | *Nose or mouth, then windpipe, then lungs | Food pipe, then lungs
